Durarara!! AU
Chapter 21 – Part 2
Lane A
Masaomi did not ask what Mikado had written.
He watched his friend’s face instead, which was worse. The late smile was gone for good now. In its place was the quiet, stubborn look of a boy rearranging a whole city around a doorway he could not stop replaying: ruined shoes in his own hand, new Nikes in Anri’s, Curtis already finished being useful, Anri saying Ryuugamine like the extra syllable had become too expensive.
“You’re going to chew that afternoon into a philosophy if I let you,” Masaomi said as they waited for a light. “Don’t. It’s shoes. It’s a weird transfer student with money and no sense of timing. That’s all.”
Mikado’s fingers tightened on the bag.
“…It didn’t feel like shoes.”
Masaomi’s mouth flattened. There it was. The first honest edge of the hunger he had been trying to keep Mikado from growing. Not just jealousy over Anri. The beginning of a belief that ordinary helpfulness was too small for Ikebukuro, and that the people who mattered were the ones who could replace a loss instead of merely finding it.
“That’s how it starts,” Masaomi muttered, mostly to himself. “Feeling like the regular version of caring isn’t enough.”
He clapped Mikado on the shoulder anyway, because the alternative was saying the rest out loud: that Curtis was exactly the kind of gravity that turned kind boys into people who went looking for a bigger darkness to stand in.

Lane B
Russia Sushi was already loud in the way Simon’s place was always loud: steel, rice, Russian over Japanese, the comfort of a room that did not care what kind of myth you were becoming outside it.
Curtis arrived first.
Gift bag on the counter. Uniform still on. Long black braids neat against the unrested line of his shoulders. He took a seat with his back to a wall from habit and watched the door like a man attending a dinner that had already been interrupted by firecrackers and was likely to be interrupted again.
Celty came next.
Yellow helmet under one arm, black riding suit stark in the restaurant light, she stopped for half a second when she saw him already there. The private history of the last two days occupied the space between them without permission: open door, drowned phone, white scythe, blood, get out now, the tower, the apology, the restored number. None of it could be said here. All of it was present.
Her real voice reached him anyway, low through the private channel.
“…Shinra invited you.”
Not a question. A placement.
Curtis tipped his head once.
“He did. Then Yagiri exploded in miniature and we got sushi instead.”
Before Celty could answer that, Mei arrived.
Pink hair. Sharp eyes. The easy unhurried posture of someone who had not been summoned so much as consented to appear. She took in the helmet, the foreigner, the gift bag, and the fact that Celty had stopped just inside the door like the room now contained one extra variable too many.
Curtis stood.
Not formal to the point of theater. Just the book-learned politeness Anri had already noticed.
“Curtis Nightstar.”
Mei’s mouth curved, interested and unreadable.
“Mei. Shinra’s old classmate. You’re the one he keeps mentioning like a fascinating bruise.” Her gaze moved over his braids, his height, the tired calm that did not match a first-year transfer student. “So you’re real. Good. I was starting to think he invented you for conversation.”
Celty’s black smoke wanted to rise and had nowhere public to go.
Curtis read the table as it formed.
Celty was braced.
Mei was comfortable in a way that did not ask Celty’s permission.
The two women knew each other well enough that the air between them already had history.
Shinra was still missing, which meant the first meeting would happen without his delighted narration to blur the edges.
What Curtis could not correctly name yet was the heat under Mei’s ease.
He filed the first version anyway, because his brain needed a category:
old intimacy.
active.
not hidden hard enough.
something occupying Shinra’s life in a shape Celty was not inside of.
Not quite rival.
Not quite guest.
The closest models he had were affair, leftover relationship, or a woman waiting in a vacancy that had not been admitted.
He was already slightly wrong.
He was also not imagining the current.
Mei slid onto a seat like she belonged there and looked from Celty to Curtis with bright, lazy precision.
“Well,” she said. “This should be educational.”
Simon boomed a greeting from behind the counter. A fortune cookie wait somewhere in the later hour, still unopened. Outside, Yagiri Pharmaceuticals was still dealing with a boy and a string of firecrackers. Inside Russia Sushi, the two originals of the AU finally sat in the same room, and Celty had to watch Curtis begin to study Mei with the same quiet attention he used on crime scenes, grief, and people who thought they were hiding more than they were.
Curtis lifted a hand toward the counter.
The smile he gave Simon was real enough in intention and faint enough in execution that nobody at the table quite caught it. A small courtesy from a boy who had eaten here before, buried under the larger work of reading the room. Simon boomed back anyway, already treating the tall foreigner as a returning problem worth feeding.
Then the air close to Mei registered.
Curtis’s mismatched eyes did not change. His posture did not change. Only the filing system behind his face tightened. He knew that smell. Not from clubs, not from rumor, not from some abstract idea of adult entanglement. From walking back into rooms he had shared with Justice and catching the leftover heat of a night that had not belonged to him: skin, sex, semen, the particular lived-in evidence of bodies that had finished with each other and not yet bothered to fully erase it. Up close, on Mei, it was unmistakable. Under it, fainter but present, was Shinra.
The category clicked in the only shape Curtis currently owned.
Not colleague.
Not a clean visit.
Recent. Physical. Shinra’s.
Long black braids shifted against his uniform as he sat back down, polite interest still arranged on his face while the detective part of him revised the table. Celty could not smell it. That fact landed with a dry, ugly usefulness. She was sitting in the same air as evidence she had no biological access to, while Curtis had just been handed a piece of the household Shinra had called fine because Celty could not catch it.
Mei noticed the fractional stillness in him.
Not the smile toward Simon. The other thing. The way his attention had sharpened without becoming rude.
Curtis let her see only the surface: tired transfer student, gift bag at his knee, a boy invited to dinner after firecrackers rerouted the evening. Inside, the first wrong-and-right conclusion had already formed.
Shinra and Mei were not only old classmates.
Whatever they were, it had been close enough, recently enough, that the body kept the record even when the conversation did not.
He turned his glass a quarter-inch on the counter and said nothing.
Some evidence did not need to be spoken at a sushi counter to start changing the night.
Curtis turned the question into the room as if he had not just rewritten Mei’s place at the table by smell alone. Long black braids shifted against his uniform, tone easy, almost dry.
“So. What kind of odd things has Shinra said about me?” A short pause. “To be honest, I only met him once.”
Mei’s mouth curved, delighted by the opening.
“Only once? That’s cute. He talks about you like you’ve been a recurring specimen for months.” She ticked items off with two fingers, unhurried. “Tall foreigner Shizuo dragged in. Unreasonably calm about being stabbed. Scar map he was instructed not to comment on. Blood type that made him light up like a child at a crime scene. And something about Appalachia that he keeps circling as if the word itself might consent to dissection if he says it enough.”
Celty’s helmet stayed on the counter, but the private channel cut toward Curtis at once, low and warning.
He would say all of that.
Mei leaned her cheek into her hand, still watching him.
“He also said you left too quickly. Which, from Shinra, means he didn’t get to ask the questions he actually wanted.” A thinner smile. “So. One meeting, and you’re already a bruise he keeps pressing. That tracks.”
Curtis gave a small polite nod, as if this were only gossip and not confirmation that his body, his blood, and his exits had already become stories in a room he had never agreed to enter.
“Yeah,” he said. “That sounds like him.”
He did not add the rest: that the woman recounting Shinra’s fascination still smelled like Shinra, and that this dinner was no longer only about what the doctor had said.
It was about what the doctor had been doing before he got called away to firecrackers.
Shinra arrived like the firecracker incident had been a stimulating errand instead of a reason to cancel dinner.
The door chimed. He crossed the restaurant with his coat still half-settled and his glasses slightly askew, scanned the counter, and lit up the instant he saw the tall foreigner already seated between Celty and Mei with a gift bag at his knee.
“Curtis!”
The delight was immediate and unfeigned. He clapped a hand once, as if the reroute to Russia Sushi had been a stroke of social genius rather than a lab emergency with explosives attached.
“You actually came. I was prepared for you to evaporate into the city again and leave me with only secondhand bruises and Celty’s silence.” He dropped onto the remaining seat, still talking. “Yagiri was briefly theatrical — firecrackers, a boy screaming about a missing girl, Yoshida making faces — but the important work is already in motion. More importantly, you’re here. One meeting was an insult to curiosity. This is much better.”
His gaze flicked from Curtis to Mei to Celty, pleased with the composition of the table in a way that made Celty’s private channel go dangerously still.
“You’ve met Mei, then. Good. I told her you were worth looking at twice. And you brought a gift. Of course you did. Polite and abnormal. My favorite combination.”
Curtis offered a faint nod, long black braids shifting against his uniform.
“Shinra.”
The doctor beamed, already leaning in as if the sushi counter were an examination tray.
“Don’t look so guarded. This is dinner, not a dissection. Although if you faint or bleed or do that alarming recovery thing again, I reserve the right to take notes.” He signaled Simon without looking away from Curtis. “Eat. Talk. Exist in one place long enough for me to confirm you’re not a rumor Shizuo invented to make my week more interesting.”
Mei watched Shinra’s brightness land on Curtis and did not bother hiding her amusement.
Celty sat inside the same brightness and felt the room get smaller.
Curtis kept his face polite, the scent-read on Mei still filed and unspoken, while Shinra settled in as if nothing at the table were already compromised except, possibly, Curtis’s privacy.
Shinra was still talking. Mei was still amused. Simon was still making the counter feel like the safest loud place in Ikebukuro.
Celty was not looking at any of that.
Her attention sat on Curtis instead, on the too-still way he held his politeness, the faint delay in his responses, the sense — not smell, not sight exactly — that something in him was running hot and misaligned under the uniform. Shinra could not sense it. Mei could read faces and missed the private frequency. Celty had the channel whether she wanted it or not.
The question reached him without moving her helmet.
Something the matter? Those two can’t sense it. But something’s off with you. Everything okay?
Curtis kept his eyes on the menu long enough to look ordinary. Long black braids shifted against his shoulder as he answered the same way she had asked, through the mind-link rather than the table.
A lot’s going on. I’m not exactly in the right headspace.
No joke. No it’s fine. No medical deflection for Shinra to seize on. Just the stripped admission of a man sitting in a rerouted dinner with a gift bag at his knee, Anri waiting on an after-hour, Mikado’s ruined shoes still echoing from the hallway, Mei’s leftover scent already filed, and Celty close enough that the last two days had no business sharing a sushi counter with any of it.
Celty’s private voice came back lower.
Then don’t perform the table harder than you have to. They’ll eat the performance and miss the rest. I won’t.
Shinra chose that exact moment to lean in, delighted, completely outside the exchange.
“So! Appalachia. Start anywhere. I have follow-up questions.”
Curtis’s mouth tugged into the faintest public shape of a smile, while under it the other conversation remained open between him and the headless woman at his side:
I know. That’s the problem.
Curtis did not give Shinra the version with cryptids, brands, or dead brothers. Not at a sushi counter. Not with Mei’s attention already too sharp and Celty sitting close enough to hear both the public sentence and the private weather under it.
He turned his chopsticks a fraction, long black braids shifting against his uniform, and answered like a man reducing a forest to two usable facts.
“What about it? I got abandoned in a cabin. Then wandered around in the forest.”
The table took that in four different ways.
Shinra’s eyes brightened immediately, the medical curiosity flaring so fast it almost became affection.
“Yes. Yes, that tracks with the first meeting. Isolated origin, prolonged exposure, scar distribution that does not belong to a single accident. A cabin implies a point of last human contact. The wandering implies duration. How old were you when the cabin stopped being occupied? Days or seasons in the trees after that? Any structured survival, or purely feral adaptation?” He caught himself, barely. “You don’t have to answer all of that at once. I am simply establishing a timeline.”
Mei watched Curtis over the rim of her glass, less clinical than Shinra and somehow more invasive for it.
“Abandoned in a cabin,” she repeated, as if tasting the phrase. “That’s a very clean sentence for an ugly thing. You say it like you’re reading it off a form.”
Celty did not speak out loud. The private channel did.
That’s the version you give rooms you don’t trust yet. I already heard a fuller one. Don’t let him turn the cabin into a specimen drawer in front of her.
Curtis kept his face polite.
“I was five,” he said, because Shinra already had pieces of this and lying smaller would only make the doctor dig. “The cabin wasn’t a home for long. After that it was just woods. You already saw what the woods left.”
He let the scars stand in for the rest. No Justice. No list. No white scythe. No name he was forbidden to speak.
Shinra looked like a man who had been handed an appetizer and called it an entrée.
“Five. That’s—” He exhaled, delighted and appalled in equal measure. “The developmental implications alone. And you presented to me years later as functional, multilingual, and casually durable. Curtis, that’s not a backstory. That’s a research proposal that learned how to walk.”
Mei’s smile sharpened.
“And now he’s bringing tea to dinner. Fascinating bruise indeed.”
Curtis’s mismatched eyes stayed on the counter.
“It’s just Appalachia,” he said. “People get weird ideas about it. Most of the true part is quieter than they want.”
Which was true.
And incomplete.
And, for Russia Sushi, as far as he intended to go while the smell of Shinra still sat on Mei and Celty listened for the moment the public version started to crack.
Curtis turned the tea bag an inch with his thumb, long black braids shifting against his uniform as the stripped cabin sentence opened whether Shinra deserved the extra inches or not.
“I left the cabin to go track my parents. I tried my best to find them. But no dice.” A short pause, eyes on the counter rather than the doctor. “By evening I was tired and lost. That’s when I met Justice. Similar story — but don’t push your luck. It’s rude to talk about someone’s life when they’re not around, so I won’t tell you what happened to him or why he was abandoned.”
Mei’s brows lifted. Shinra, to his credit, actually closed his mouth on the first follow-up.
Curtis kept going anyway, because the part he would give was the part that still belonged to both of them as living boys and not as a corpse’s secret.
“He was four, but more accustomed to the forest than I was. He was left there since he was three. Survived it a whole year.” A faint, almost dry breath. “Although when I met him his English was bad, and mine was no better. But as we traveled and stole from cabins and other surrounding places, we learned to read and speak properly.”
The sushi counter went quieter around that last image: two undersized children moving through Appalachia like a failed search party that had become a survival unit, lifting food and language out of other people’s seasonal houses because no one had stayed behind to teach them either.
Shinra’s delight dimmed into something more complicated, though the curiosity did not leave.
“A year alone at three,” he murmured. “Then the two of you as a closed learning system. Theft as literacy. That’s—” He caught the warning this time and redirected, barely. “You named him. Justice. He was the other boy in the photograph.”
Curtis’s gaze flicked up, just enough to confirm and close the door behind it.
“Yeah. And he’s not here. So that’s all you get of him.”
Mei turned her glass slowly, studying Curtis with fresh interest.
“So the polite gift-bringing foreigner taught himself civilization out of stolen cabin books with another abandoned kid.” Her mouth curved. “No wonder Shinra looks at you like a thesis that learned manners.”
Celty’s private voice cut in, low and tight.
You just gave him more than the cabin. Watch how fast he tries to turn Justice into a second specimen. You already drew the line. Hold it.
Curtis picked up his chopsticks again, public face still even.
“I told you what I was willing to tell,” he said. “The forest, the cabin, how we learned words. Not his reasons. Not his ending. If that’s not enough dinner conversation, talk about firecrackers.”
Shinra looked physically pained by the restraint.
He also, for the moment, did not push.
Shinra lasted almost a full minute on firecrackers.
It was a personal record.
“The boy at Yagiri was not theatrical for sport,” he said, recovering his brightness by changing specimens. “He was telling the truth. People always assume screaming is the unreliable part. Usually it is the only honest part. Yoshida wanted the memory trimmed. I told him memories are a sticky business. They come back in the wrong rooms.” His eyes flicked to Curtis, then away again, as if the word memory had gotten too close to Appalachia. “Anyway. Sushi.”
Mei did not let the table reset that easily.
“You two learned language by stealing it,” she said to Curtis. “That’s not a cute wilderness anecdote. That’s a working method. Do you still do that? Take what a room doesn’t know it’s offering and walk out literate in it?”
Curtis met her gaze without flinching, long black braids shifting against his uniform.
“I pay for things now.”
“That wasn’t what I asked.”
Celty’s private channel cut across the space between them, low and warning.
She is testing how you read people. Do not give her the version of you that files scents and doors for free.
Curtis let a small polite shrug answer Mei instead of the real sentence: yes, I do, and I already did it to you before Shinra sat down.
“I listen,” he said. “That’s all.”
Shinra beamed, missing the current under the words.
“He does. It’s unsettling. Shizuo brought him in bleeding and he still had the presence of mind to tell me not to comment on his body. Most people in that condition just swear. He issued protocol.” He leaned in, delighted. “That photograph, though. Two boys. One of them is clearly you. The other—”
Curtis’s chopsticks stilled.
Shinra lifted both hands at once.
“Not his reasons. Not his ending. I heard you. I am merely haunted by composition. Two children in a forest is a painting. Two children who taught themselves language out of other people’s cabins is a system. I like systems.”
Simon chose that moment to land more plates with a force that reset the air. Russia Sushi became loud again. Ordinary. Almost.
Under the noise, Celty stayed on the private line.
Your headspace is still off. And you keep looking at Mei like she is evidence.
Curtis answered the same way, eyes on his plate.
She is. I just don’t have the right name for the file yet.
Across the ward, Lane A kept moving without them.
Mikado sat on a bench with the ruined shoes at his feet and Masaomi talking around the wound instead of into it. The notebook sentence was already doing its work. The version of this city where I was still necessary. Every time Anri’s new Nikes crossed his mind, the sentence got sharper. Masaomi watched that sharpening happen and hated that he recognized it. This was how boys in Ikebukuro stopped wanting to be kind and started wanting to matter at a higher, uglier altitude.
Back at the counter, Mei rested her chin in her hand and smiled at Curtis as if the dinner had finally become interesting.
“Well,” she said. “If you listen so well, tell me what this table sounds like to you.”
Shinra looked pleased.
Celty went still.
Curtis sat there with a gift bag of tea at his knee, the smell of Shinra still faintly on Mei, and a question that was no longer small talk.
He had been invited to dinner.
He was being asked to read the room out loud.
And the one category he did not own yet was sitting right beside the doctor, waiting to see whether the abandoned boy from the cabin would call it what it was — or the closest wrong thing he had.
Curtis did not give Mei the file.
He let the question hang just long enough to look considered, long black braids shifting against his uniform, then tipped his chopsticks toward the plates and the gift bag at his knee.
“Sounds like me paying and leaving soon.”
The line landed flat and practical, a door closed with courtesy still on the handle.
Shinra actually looked wounded.
“Leaving? You only just arrived. We rerouted an entire evening through firecrackers for this table and your opening move is the check?” He adjusted his glasses, already trying to turn the exit into a joke he could keep. “That is extremely rude and extremely on-brand. Sit. I have not finished being delighted.”
Mei’s mouth curved, not disappointed so much as interested in the dodge.
“So that’s what listening gets us. A bill and an escape route.” She turned her glass. “Fine. Pay if you want. But if you leave too fast, I’ll assume the table said something you didn’t want to name.”
Celty’s private voice cut in, lower than the restaurant noise.
Good. Don’t perform the read for her. She asked because she already knows you saw more than dinner.
Curtis reached for his wallet anyway, polite to the ritual even as he started building the out.
“I brought tea. I answered the cabin. That’s more than one meeting’s worth.” His mismatched eyes flicked once to Shinra, then to the door. “I’ve got somewhere after this. Late. So yeah. Sounds like me paying and leaving soon.”
He did not say Anri was the later hour.
He did not say the table smelled like Shinra on Mei.
He did not say his headspace was still a tower, a scythe, a drowned phone, and a boy with ruined shoes standing in a doorway he had not meant to own.
He only set the opening for the exit, and waited to see which of them would let him take it.
Curtis did not soften the exit. Long black braids shifted against his uniform as the politeness finally showed its edge.
“And also, I thought I came for a nice dinner. Not an interrogation.” A short, dry breath. “I could’ve gone to a workshop I’ve been meaning to go to. But instead I’m here with two lab scientists who pretend to be doctors who look like I’m fresh meat to cut open.”
The counter went still in a way Simon’s shouting from the kitchen could not quite cover.
Shinra put a hand to his chest, wounded and fascinated at once.
“Pretend? Curtis, that is slander. I am a fully realized medical professional with an unorthodox practice and an excellent bedside manner.” He paused, then smiled despite himself. “The fresh-meat part is… not entirely unfair. But I did say this was dinner, not a dissection. You keep arriving with new organs of backstory and then acting surprised when the room develops an appetite.”
Mei’s eyes lit, not offended in the least.
“Lab scientist is accurate enough,” she said. “Doctor is a costume we both wear when it helps people sit still.” She turned her glass toward him. “If you wanted a nice dinner, you brought tea to the wrong house. Shinra doesn’t know how to leave an interesting body unexamined. Neither do I. That’s not the same thing as putting you on a table.”
A thinner smile.
“Although you saying it out loud means you felt the knives anyway.”
Celty’s private channel snapped in, sharper than the public noise.
Don’t let them make you the specimen just because you sat down. You already gave the cabin. You already gave Justice’s name. If they keep pushing, leave. I will not help them turn this into another apartment examination.
Curtis held Mei’s gaze, then Shinra’s, the gift bag still at his knee like proof he had come in good faith and the room had spent it too fast.
“Then stop looking at me like the next question is hiding under my skin,” he said. “I came because I was invited. I can still go to the workshop. Both of those things are true.”
Shinra opened his mouth.
Closed it.
Opened it again, brighter and slightly chastened.
“…Fine. Eat first. Interrogate second. Or at least pretend better.”
Mei laughed under her breath, unrepentant.
“He’s not fresh meat,” she said. “He’s leftover wilderness that learned table manners. That’s worse. That’s why nobody at this counter can behave.”
The mind-link went first, stripped of table manners.
I’m going to explode. I need a smoke.
Celty felt the temperature under the sentence at once. Not performance. The actual pressure of a man who had given a cabin, a dead brother’s name, and enough of himself to two people who looked at bodies like unfinished reports.
Out loud, none of that showed.
Curtis’s face settled into something calm and almost gentle, long black braids shifting against his uniform as he turned toward Shinra with the same polite register he had used to bring tea.
“Shinra, you’re right. It would be rude for me to check out right away.” A small nod, unhurried. “Allow me to go for a smoke to calm my nerves a bit.”
The contrast was so clean it made the request sound reasonable instead of like an emergency exit with nicotine attached.
Shinra brightened, seizing the compromise.
“Yes. Yes, that’s acceptable. A pause, not a disappearance. Go. Recalibrate. Come back before the tea becomes a farewell offering.” He waved a hand toward the door, already treating the cigarette as a contained variable. “Don’t wander off and become a rumor again.”
Mei watched the polite version of him stand, unconvinced that nerves were the whole of it.
“A smoke,” she said. “How civilized. Don’t take too long, wilderness. The table’s still hungry.”
Celty’s private voice followed him as he rose.
Go. I’ll keep them off the next incision until you come back. If you keep walking after the cigarette, tell me. Don’t just vanish and leave me in this room with them.
Curtis picked up nothing but his pack, left the gift bag on the seat like a promise he intended to honor, and headed for the door with that same gentle calm still arranged on his face.
Under it, the other sentence remained:
I’m going to explode.
Russia Sushi’s noise closed behind him. The street air hit his lungs like the first honest thing the dinner had offered. He put a cigarette to his mouth, lit it, and stood outside Simon’s place as a tall foreigner on a smoke break — while inside, two doctors and a headless woman sat with the version of him that had not yet come back in.
Outside, Curtis smoked.
Inside, Mei did not wait for him to come back before making the table worse.
“Shinra, you should’ve told me he was handsome.” She said it lightly, chin in her hand, eyes still on the door he had used. “I just might have to ask where he lives and give a full body physical later, if you catch my drift.”
Shinra laughed.
Not a polite cover laugh. An actual delighted one, as if Mei turning his fascinating bruise into a proposition were simply another charming data point.
“That does sound like you,” he said. “Though I should warn you, he issues protocol when people stare at his body. Very firm about it. You’d have to be sneakier than usual.”
Celty could not eat.
There was no plate in front of her that mattered. No fortune cookie yet. No way to occupy her hands with food the way the two of them could sit there chewing through a joke that put Curtis’s body on offer like leftover sushi. She sat helmetless in the sense that the helmet was on the counter, headless in the fact that never changed, and the private reaction that filled the space where a face would have been was immediate and ugly with jealousy.
No.
Not later.
Not his address.
Not a “physical.”
The thought arrived so hard it almost became smoke in public. She had already been in that flat. She had already seen the scar map he did not show people. She had already been ordered out, followed him, watched him vanish off a tower, and held him when the forbidden truth put blood on the floor. Mei did not get to smell the room, laugh with Shinra, and then collect the foreigner’s address like a nightcap.
Celty’s inner voice stayed locked off the public table and viciously clear to herself.
She is not getting that address.
She is not putting her hands on him under the excuse of medicine.
Shinra can laugh. I am not laughing.
The possessiveness startled her even as it formed. She had told Curtis she had not come to be his replacement soft landing. She had also left his door with the invitation still open. Watching Mei aim at the same body he had only barely trusted her with made both of those facts feel suddenly insufficient.
She typed nothing.
She ate nothing.
She sat in a restaurant she could not participate in the ordinary way and decided, with quiet headless fury, that if Mei asked Curtis where he lived when he came back in from that cigarette, Celty would shut the question down before the answer left his mouth.
Curtis took the last drag outside Russia Sushi and let the smoke leave him toward the street instead of the door.
“…Alright. Keep your cool.” The words were for himself, low under the city noise. “Simon and Denis have been nothing but good to you. Don’t do anything in their store.”
He killed the cigarette, rolled his shoulders once, and put the polite face back on like a uniform layer. Long black braids settled against his back. Whatever had been close to exploding at the table — interrogation, specimen-eyes, Mei’s unreadable heat, Shinra’s appetite for origin stories — stayed outside with the ash. The restaurant was not the place to let it out. Not in a room that had fed him before he had a myth attached to his name.
He went back inside.
The bell over the door gave him away. Shinra looked up first, delighted that the smoke break had not become another vanishing. Mei’s gaze followed, still interested, still too comfortable. Celty’s attention hit him hardest, private and tight, the residue of a conversation he had not been present for sitting on her like held smoke.
Curtis crossed to his seat, gift bag still where he had left it, and sat down with the same calm he had used to ask for the cigarette.
“I’m back,” he said simply. “Still here. Still not a dissection.”
He did not know Mei had already asked, in so many words, for his address and his body.
Celty did.
And the first thing she decided, watching him take his place again among people who could eat and joke and reach for him in ways she could not plate or swallow, was that the next sentence out of Mei’s mouth would not be allowed to become an invitation.
Curtis had barely settled back onto the stool when Mei turned toward him with the same lazy precision she had used on his backstory.
“There you are. Shinra failed to mention a few relevant details.” Her smile was pleasant. Too pleasant. “For example, where a person would even find you if they wanted to continue a conversation somewhere quieter than Simon’s. Or take a more thorough look at that famous scar map of yours.”
Celty moved first.
Not with smoke in public. With the PDA, turned just enough for Mei to see the screen, and with a presence that made the counter feel suddenly narrower.
Celty:
No.
The single word sat on the display like a closed gate.
Mei blinked, then laughed under her breath.
“No? I didn’t even finish the sentence.”
Celty typed again, faster.
Celty:
You don’t need his address.
You don’t need a look at his body.
This is dinner.
Shinra lifted both hands, still smiling, already trying to turn the temperature back into a joke.
“Now, now. Mei was only being Mei. And Celty is only being Celty. Nobody is abducting the guest between plates.”
Curtis looked from the PDA to Mei, long black braids shifting against his uniform. He had missed the exact line while he was outside. He did not miss the shape of it. Address. Body. The same file he had already started on her, only aimed back at him.
The mind-link opened from his side, low.
What did she say.
Celty’s answer came immediately, stripped of diplomacy.
She wants your address. She joked about a full body physical. Shinra laughed. I am not letting that become real.
Curtis’s public face stayed even. Inside, the cabin boy, the man who had just told himself not to do anything in Simon and Denis’s store, and the person who had already shown Celty too much of that scar map all occupied the same breath.
He turned to Mei with polite finality.
“I don’t give my address out at dinner. And nobody’s doing a physical.” A short pause. “If Shinra already told you about the scars, that’s more than enough looking.”
Mei rested her chin in her hand, unoffended and not done.
“Possessive table,” she said, glancing at Celty, then back to Curtis. “Interesting. I ask one practical question and suddenly there are rules.”
Celty typed without looking away from her.
Celty:
Yes.
There are.
Shinra, still delighted and still behind, signaled for more tea as if etiquette could sand the edge off what had just been claimed.
Curtis let his fingers rest against the gift bag at his knee. He had come for a nice dinner. He was leaving soon. He had Anri after this. And now Celty had publicly, if silently, put a boundary around him in front of the woman whose skin still carried Shinra’s night.
He did not explode.
He did not walk out yet.
He sat there in Russia Sushi and understood that the table was no longer only reading him.
It was starting to fight over who was allowed to.